      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nKey information:\n- OIML Member State: SLOVAKIA\n- OIML Certificate No.: R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\n- Issued under Scheme A\n- Issuing Authority: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), Address: Hviezdoslavova 1124/31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia, Person responsible: Peter Vook, Director\n- Applicant: UAB „Axioma Metering\", Veterinaru str. 52, Biruliskes, LT-54469 Kaunas region, Lithuania\n- Manufacturer: same\n- Certified type: Water meter type Qalcosonic W1\n- Module designation: Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy class 2\n- Type evaluation report: No. 2022/ER010/SK1 dated 13th January 2023, 17 pages\n- Documentation file: \"Technical documentation file AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\" dated 13th January 2023, 114 pages\n- Certificate history: Revision 0, 13 January 2023, Certificate first issued; Revision 1, 25 May 2023, Point 2.4 Software specification – supplemented specification for all DNs separately\n- Date: signature date 25th May 2023 (Rev. 1). Date issued — the certificate history shows first issued 13 January 2023, revision 1 dated 25 May 2023. The certificate number includes 2023.02. The date_issued for the current revision would be 25 May 2023 (date of signature).\n\nPage total: 12 pages (TK-P05-85 — Page 1 of 12 pages)\n\nTK-P05-85 might be a project number / form number. The rules mention project_number. \"TK-P05-85\" appears as a footer — likely the issuing authority's form template number. Hmm, could be interpreted as a form ID. I'll include it as project_number? It's risky. The schema has `project_number` for certificate. TK-P05-85 is a form/template code (V03/23-01-10 is the version). I could include it as project_number since it's the only identifier like that. I'll include it.\n\nDescription:\n- Ultrasonic water meter QALCOSONIC W1 measures volume of water at metering conditions; compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device; bidirectional transit-time principle; can be installed in horizontal and vertical positions; not designed to measure reverse flow.\n\nParts:\n- Flow sensor (component)\n- Calculator and indication device (component)\n- Display: upper line 9 digits, indication range 999999.999 m³; bottom line 5 digits for flow rate m³/h\n- Batteries: non-replaceable lithium, life 16 years\n  - For DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32: two internal \"AA\" size batteries\n  - (Second bullet seems to be a typo variant with + one \"A23\")\n  - DN40: one internal \"D\" size battery\n  - DN40: one \"D\" + one \"A23\"\n- Non-essential: antenna; non-return valve – optionally\n\nModel family: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40 mentioned in battery configurations. These are nominal diameters. The cert covers multiple DN variants. Should I create model_family with models DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40? The battery configurations vary by DN. The fragment mentions DNs in the battery section. Since attributes vary per DN (battery config), model_level characteristics could include battery life (16 years, type-level), battery_configuration as config_level or model_level per DN.\n\nI think it's reasonable to include model_family with models: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40 and model_level attribute for battery configuration. But careful: the fragment lists DNs only in the battery bullet list, and there's a typo (first two bullets both say DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 but second says \"+ one 'A23'\"). I'll extract battery configurations as model_level attribute \"battery_configuration\" with values per model. Actually the second bullet is likely for \"DN40\" or... The text: \n- for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 two internal baterries „AA:size\";\n- for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32: two internal batteries „AA\" size + one „A23\";\n- for DN40 one internal battery „D\" size;\n- for DN40 one internal battery „D\" size + one \"A23\".\n\nThis looks like OCR duplication of two variants (probably two battery options per DN group). So for DN15–DN32: option 1: two AA; option 2: two AA + one A23. For DN40: option 1: one D; option 2: one D + one A23.\n\nI could record this in model_level as \"battery_configuration\" with values listing the options, or config_level. Simplest: type_level characteristic battery_life = 16 years. And model_level for battery_configuration with values {model: \"DN15-DN32\", value: \"two internal 'AA' size batteries OR two 'AA' + one 'A23'\"} — hmm. Maybe config_level with axis \"battery_option\". Let me keep it simpler: model_level attribute \"battery_configuration\" with values per model group:\n\nvalues: [\n {model: \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", value: \"two internal 'AA' size batteries (option: two 'AA' + one 'A23')\"},\n {model: \"DN40\", value: \"one internal 'D' size battery (option: one 'D' + one 'A23')\"}\n]\n\nBut strict rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. For string values, unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null. In the R60 example in the rules, the model_level entries don't show unit_id on values... The example shows {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25} without unit objects at the value level, and unit_symbol at the attribute level. Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with shape {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For model_level, the examples put unit at attribute level and value plain. I'll follow the example format for model_level: {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values\": [{\"model\", \"value\"}]}.\n\nFor battery configuration, since it's a string value:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"battery_configuration\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN15\", \"value\": \"...\"}, ...]}\n\nHmm, but models are DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 — same config. I could group as one entry per model. Let me produce:\n\nvalues: [\n  {\"model\": \"DN15\", \"value\": \"two internal \\\"AA\\\" size batteries; option: two \\\"AA\\\" size + one \\\"A23\\\"\"},\n  {\"model\": \"DN20\", \"value\": same},\n  {\"model\": \"DN25\", \"value\": same},\n  {\"model\": \"DN32\", \"value\": same},\n  {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"one internal \\\"D\\\" size battery; option: one \\\"D\\\" size + one \\\"A23\\\"\"}\n]\n\nThat's fine.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- display_upper_line_digits: 9 (digits) — unit \"digits\"\n- display_upper_line_indication_range: {min: 0, max: 999999.999}? The indication range of 999999.999 m³ — value could be 999999.999, unit \"m³\". \n- display_bottom_line_digits: 5, shows current flow rate in m³/h\n- battery_life: 16 years\n- battery_type: \"non-replaceable lithium\"\n\nComponents:\n- role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [], characteristics: description of construction? Maybe just role and description-like fields. Schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can add a characteristics field with descriptive entries, but structured values needed... Components characteristics{} — I can put strings. Let me keep minimal: role \"Flow sensor\" with description in characteristics maybe. Actually characteristics{} is free-form per schema (sub-characteristics). I'll include descriptive content as strings.\n\nHmm, for flow sensor there are no named type designations. Calculator and indication device — directly mounted. Non-essential parts: antenna, non-return valve (optional). Could add components for those with role \"non-essential part\".\n\nMaybe keep components minimal:\n- {role: \"Flow sensor\", type_designations: []}\n- {role: \"Calculator and indication device\", type_designations: []}\n\nOr skip components since no type designations named? The schema says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The cert names \"Flow sensor\" and \"Calculator and indication device\" as essential parts. I'll include them with descriptive characteristics.\n\nModule designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\" → module_designation. The 